          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is osteoarthritis, and is it the same thing as arthritis?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is a type of joint disorder that involves the entire joint, including the cartilage, underlying bone, ligaments, joint lining, and surrounding muscles. In osteoarthritis, the cartilage gradually breaks down, leading to changes in the bone beneath it as well. It is commonly found in the knees, hips, hands, and spine. This condition is often mischaracterized as \"wear and tear,\" but it actually involves an active inflammatory process and attempts at repair, making exercises and activity important for management (source: General clinical reference).\n\nOn the other hand, the term \"arthritis\" is a broader category that encompasses over 100 different types of joint inflammation, including osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and others. While osteoarthritis is a specific condition within this category, arthritis refers to inflammation of the joints and can involve varying symptoms and mechanisms depending on the specific type. \n\nIn summary, osteoarthritis is a specific type of arthritis, characterized mainly by the degeneration of cartilage, while arthritis as a general term refers to a range of conditions affecting the joints.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
